# Patch 0004 - generated Swift async runtime: one owner for the raw future handle (LOCAL, offered upstream)
#
# Upstream base: boltffi v0.30.1, tag commit 2e6320a6d92cb591d22b908477f3a47da7ebc9bc.
#   Re-checked 2026-09-16 against upstream main d5eba2e347a957a7fce67bb738ae37d985ba082b:
#   `git diff 2e6320a6 origin/main -- boltffi_backend/templates/target/swift/` is
#   empty, so this defect is both unreleased and unfixed upstream. PR #732 (the
#   Kotlin/Java/C# close-race PR ported as patch 0002) does not touch this file.
#   Upstream has already fixed one instance of this same defect class and named
#   it: commit b01038ef35f50bd1512a99080d8541e9cfd4fa48 ("do not double-drive
#   async poll Ready via return and callback"), an ancestor of v0.30.1 and of
#   main. The owner/lifetime half of the class is what remains open.
#
# Defect (reproduced, not hypothesised). The generated Swift driver called
#   `rust_future_free` from three places: the Ready path (after `complete`), the
#   error path, and both branches of the cancellation handler - and it re-armed a
#   poll by spawning an unstructured `Task { await Task.yield(); start() }`. Two
#   concrete use-after-frees follow from that, and both are visible from the
#   runtime's own source:
#
#   A. Queued re-poll after the free. `RustFuture::poll` parks the continuation
#      and later the runtime wakes it with `RustFuturePoll::MaybeReady`
#      (continuation.rs `wake()` -> `Policy::wake()`). Swift's `handle(1)` then
#      scheduled `Task { await Task.yield(); start() }` - a queued re-poll that
#      the caller's cancellation cannot cancel. If the caller's task is cancelled
#      in that window, `onCancel` runs `state.finish()` -> `rust_future_cancel`
#      -> `rust_future_free`, and the queued re-poll then calls `rust_future_poll`
#      with a handle whose `Arc<RustFuture>` has already been consumed by
#      `consume_future` (`Arc::from_raw` + drop). Every later borrow
#      (`with_future` / `with_future_arc` = `Arc::from_raw` + `mem::forget`) is a
#      use-after-free. This is the shape the real simulator suite exposed.
#
#   B. Free inside a native call. `RustFuture::poll` invokes the continuation
#      callback synchronously - on the Ready path, on the cancelled path, and
#      from `store_continuation` when it displaces a parked continuation - so the
#      Ready branch of the old Swift driver ran `complete` *and* `free` while the
#      runtime was still inside `poll` with a live `&Arc<RustFuture>`, and, in the
#      displacement case, before `store_continuation` wrote the next continuation
#      into that future (the window b01038ef's message describes). A free from
#      inside a callback also drops the boxed user future while the runtime's
#      `Mutex<FutureExecutionState>` may still be in use.
#
# Observational evidence: PR #19 run 35089590460 (head 6e0922f) and run
#   35092248876 (head 5277148) both executed the real simulator suite against the
#   real Rust library: the contract suite passed and printed
#   `BOLT_PROOF version=0.1.0 sender=PASS viewer=PASS typed_errors=PASS
#   layout=PASS`, then the run died with "Test crashed with signal segv before
#   starting test execution" on `BoltOwnership.repeatedCancellation()`.
#
# What this change does. The handle gets exactly one owner,
#   `BoltFFIFutureLifetime`, which holds it (in an immutable `Owner` box) plus the
#   `@Sendable` free function, and issues the free once, from `deinit`:
#
#   * `deinit` runs exactly once (ARC), so repeated cancellation, a dropped
#     re-poll and the error path can never double free or leak a free.
#   * The free is *enqueued* onto the call's serial `DispatchQueue` rather than
#     called inline, so it also cannot run inside a native call or inside a
#     callback the runtime makes into this module.
#   * Every poll and the cancel are issued on that same queue, and each queued
#     block owns its issuer (the driver, which owns the lifetime). The handle can
#     therefore not be released while a poll is in flight, and the last queued
#     poll always runs before the free - which is exactly invariant A.
#   * A re-poll triggered by a wake is queued instead of being run from inside the
#     runtime's frame, and the unstructured `Task` is gone (it also escaped the
#     caller's cancellation domain, which is why a cancellation could not stop
#     it). Instead of a second overlapping poll, this side now has at most one
#     poll in flight, so it never displaces the continuation the runtime parked
#     for it - the one path where the runtime writes to a future after invoking a
#     continuation.
#   * The cancellation handler marks the native future cancelled (queued) and
#     resumes the caller; it never frees while a poll, a callback or a queued
#     re-poll can still dereference the handle.
#
# Correction to an earlier draft of this patch: enqueuing merely the *poll* (and
#   freeing inline from the callback) is NOT sufficient - the wake path delivers
#   `MaybeReady` on a native thread, so the driver's last release, and with it a
#   deinit-time free, would have happened inside the runtime's callback frame.
#   The free has to be deferred onto the same queue that issues the native calls,
#   which is what this patch does, and the host probe asserts it (a free observed
#   while a native call or callback is on the stack is recorded as a violation).
#
# Why not the other candidates:
#   * another `canPoll()` check before re-polling is still check-then-act: the
#     re-poll can be queued before the cancellation wins and run after it;
#   * freeing in the cancellation handler after `rust_future_cancel` has the same
#     ordering problem and additionally frees from a native thread;
#   * a blocking lock (e.g. holding a lock across the native call or a
#     read-write lock around the free) converts a memory-safety defect into a hang
#     risk - UniFFI's Kotlin object template explicitly rejects that trade
#     (`ObjectTemplate.kt`, "destroy inside a read/write lock is a hidden
#     blocking hazard");
#   * `Unmanaged.passRetained`-style single-owner shortcuts cannot cover a queued
#     block that is still going to run, which is the defect.
#
# Acceptance answers, per the task's requirements for a non-upstream modification:
#   1. Invariant violated - the handle had several independent disposers (Ready
#      path, error path, both cancellation branches) while a poll could still be
#      queued, and one of those disposers ran inside the runtime's own frame.
#   2. Why this restores it - one ARC owner; every actor that can still reach the
#      handle holds that owner (the poll driver, each queued poll block, the
#      cancellation path); polls and the cancel are serialised; the free is
#      enqueued behind them and runs once, from `deinit`.
#   3. Test that fails if removed - `host/LifetimeProbe.swift`
#      (`BoltAsyncLifetime` suite) drives the generated runtime against a model of
#      the Rust contract (borrowed poll/cancel/complete, consuming free) and
#      records "poll after free", "free inside a native call", "free inside a
#      native callback", "double free" and the free count. It fails on the
#      pre-0004 runtime (the Ready path frees inside the poll frame: deterministic)
#      and passes with it. The Apple job runs that probe as its own RED/GREEN pair
#      (`--exclude 0004`), and the real-Rust suite adds the empirical SIGSEGV.
#   4. Generalization - every BoltFFI Swift consumer that cancels an async call
#      has this window; the change is in the shared generator template, so it
#      covers all of them, and Greenfield5's own session API is class-shaped and
#      hits it directly.
#   5. New assumption and its enforcement - (a) the free is ordered after every
#      native call this side issues: enforced by the serial queue, asserted by the
#      probe's in-native-call detector; (b) the runtime's wake path does not touch
#      the `RustFuture` allocation after invoking a parked continuation
#      (continuation.rs `wake()` -> `invoke_stored()` -> return, all scheduler
#      state that is separately `Arc`-owned), so an enqueued free that racemakes
#      the wake frame's tail unwind is safe; (c) the runtime tolerates a poll of a
#      cancelled future (it short-circuits on `is_cancelled` and answers Ready) -
#      asserted upstream by `async_cancellation.rs` and used here so that a
#      stranded re-poll is harmless; (d) exactly-once delivery of a parked
#      continuation is the runtime's own atomic state machine, and the probe
#      asserts the retain balance from the foreign side (every retained driver is
#      consumed exactly once).
#   6. Maintenance - one file, two small internal classes; delete it as soon as
#      upstream ships an equivalent lifetime discipline. Recorded upstream-ready,
#      no upstream issue or PR filed from this task.
#
# Upstream test that would prevent regression: `boltffi_tests/tests/async_cancellation.rs`
#   covers cancel / repeated cancel / free on unpolled, completed and cancelled
#   futures, but never (i) a foreign runtime that re-polls after the free, nor
#   (ii) a free invoked from inside the continuation callback. A test that parks a
#   continuation, frees the handle from inside the Ready delivery, and then polls
#   the same handle - plus a test that asserts a second `rust_future_poll` while
#   one continuation is parked does not displace it into a foreign free - belongs
#   next to `cancel_stops_further_polling`.
#
diff --git a/boltffi_backend/templates/target/swift/async.swift b/boltffi_backend/templates/target/swift/async.swift
--- a/boltffi_backend/templates/target/swift/async.swift
